Any MS-Windows plain text editor that can write Unix
style line ends is fine, you probably already have a
programmer's editor of some sort. You just won't be
able to edit directly under Cygwin in its native
windows. The Cygwin installation still exists within
your MS-Windows file system as a perfectly normal
set of files, so you can navigate to a file with
MS-Windows' internal Explorer, open it in your
plain text editor of choice, then edit files from
MS-Windows and compile them from Cygwin. I'd hate
the brokenness that puts into the integration, but
it would work. Doing that in any case gives you the
chance to edit a file, never leave the editor, save
your work, compile that work, find where fixes or
additions are needed, without the overhead of
opening and closing edit sessions all the time.

By the way, "rxvt &" is your friend. The Cygwin startup
window is an MS-DOS window, with all the crippling that
implies. rxvt looks much like a typical Linux terminal
window. Use "man" and "info" early and often.

You should have vim, "Vi IMproved" installed already,
it's built in help is wonderful, but the learning curve
is more like a learning wall. Modal editors are an
acquired taste, but after using the several flavors of
vi since 1984, you'll take vim from me only when I'm
cold and dead.  Emacs is _not_ installed by default,
but it can be gotten by another run of setup.

Whether to use vi/vim/stevie/elvis or
emacs/xemacs/memacs is a religious issue, they offer
very similar functionality. Vim's downside is that
it's modal, emacs' downside is that the finger you
use to press the meta key will eventually be strong
enough to benchpress small automobiles. Memacs
exists in part to overcome the horrible "huge
footprint" problem of standard emacs, vim exists
both to do that and because vi goes back to the
origins of Unix, but on the gripping hand, emacs
exists because a *nix sysadmin can fire it up and do
a whole days' work without ever clicking into
another window -- emacs sort of _subsumes_ little
meaningless concepts like "operating system" or
"shell program".

Both vim and emacs have simply splendid facilities
that integrate debugging (some low typing cost
flavor of "go to next error message" takes you to
the message and to the place in the code where it
occurred) function call following (read "man
ctags"), and executable building directly into the
editor (either lets you execute any command and
capture its (text) output into the current text
(sub)window or into a new window), but you'll
probably learn those only over time. You'll save
lots of pain if "over time" is "as early as
possible", though.

Vim has a really, really wonderful "keyboard text
macro" capture facility that lets you do really
complicated things (it's a "Turing complete"
language in itself, which means it can do "anything
any programming language can do" (someone has
written a "Towers of Hanoi" toy entirely in vim's
macro language, e.g.), but you are limited to around
60 macros because they are tied to single typed
characters. There is also an entire bigger
idiosyncratic programming language in which to write
complex functions that then integrate right into the
language via its "internal command line" execution
mechanism. Vim can be tailored to do many, many
things specific to the language in which you are
writing software, like syntax highlighting,
delimiter recognition, and syntax oriented
formatting.

Emacs has a somewhat clunkier keyboard macro capture
capability, but with more available macros because
it supports longer macro names, and has its own more
capable fairly standard built in programming
language, elisp ("emacs lisp"), a Turing complete
form of the lisp/scheme/ml programming language
family.  There are countless elisp programs
available for download to do pretty much anything.
Emacs also can do smart syntax highlighting, et
cetera.

Most *nix systems programmers seem to use emacs,
most *nix applications programmers seem to use vim,
but that's probably an over-generalization.

Doing either is entirely optional, recommended, but
could be gathered in while coding in some plain text
programming language with which you are already
acquainted, so as not to slow down your C learning.

